COACH: No worries at all, Jonathan — that's exactly what this session is for! Let me explain it.

A salt is a random string that bcrypt generates and adds to your password before hashing it. So instead of hashing just "password123", bcrypt hashes something like "password123$2b$10$randomcharacters".

This is important for two reasons:
1. Two users with the same password will get different hashes — so attackers can't look at the database and spot matching passwords.
2. It defeats "rainbow table" attacks — where hackers use pre-computed lists of common password hashes to crack them quickly.

The number 10 you passed into bcrypt.genSalt(10) is called the cost factor — it controls how slow the hashing process is. Slower means harder for attackers to crack.

